Current target architecture:

DELTACAST input
  -> OBS capture/encode
  -> Local stream URL
  -> ASP.NET Core recorder control
  -> FFmpeg segmented recording with -c copy
  -> Browser control and optional preview

Important design rule:

The browser must not be the recording encoder when we need to preserve OBS codec, FPS, and bitrate. Browser MediaRecorder re-encodes. The backend recorder uses FFmpeg with stream copy so the saved segments keep the OBS encoded stream.

Recording flow:

1. OBS receives video from DELTACAST.
2. OBS streams to a local RTMP/SRT/HLS-capable media endpoint.
3. ASP.NET Core starts FFmpeg against the OBS stream URL.
4. FFmpeg writes 2-minute files to Emerald.Streaming\Recordings.
5. The browser polls recorder status and lists saved segments.

Preview flow:

The browser can preview only browser-playable formats. RTMP is not directly playable in normal browser video tags. Use an HLS preview URL, WebRTC preview, or another browser-compatible output if preview is needed.

Production note:

If uninterrupted recording matters, FFmpeg should run as a backend process/service. Browser refreshes should not affect recording. The current OBS recorder follows that approach: once the backend FFmpeg process starts, refreshing the web UI does not stop the recording.
